<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Datasets.</span> To ensure the generalizability of the proposed VPFC method, we evaluated it on a variety of benchmarks encompassing both discriminative tasks (e.g., POPE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023c</a>]</cite> and MME<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2023</a>]</cite>) and generative tasks (e.g., CHAIR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rohrbach et al., 2018</a>]</cite> and LLaVA-Bench-in-the-wild<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>]</cite>). Further details can be found in Supplementary Material B.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline Selection.</span> We adopt VCD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leng et al., 2024</a>]</cite>, a well-established hallucination mitigation method, alongside two recently introduced State-of-the-Art approaches, SID<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huo et al., 2025</a>]</cite> and Memory-Space Visual Retracing (MemVR)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2025</a>]</cite>, as experimental baselines to facilitate a fair comparison with our proposed method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> We use LLaVA-v1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>]</cite> and Qwen-VL-7B<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>]</cite> as the MLLM backbones. The enhancement factor, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, is set to 4, and the proportion of selected attention heads, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, is set to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Greedy search is used as the decoding strategy in all experiments.</p>
</div>
